[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a practical, step-by-step guide that answers the three things you asked about:

1. **Do you need vector tiles for 8 000 trail segments, or can you just use GeoJSON?**  
2. **How to add a terrain-and-roads basemap in MapLibre GL JS.**  
3. **A minimal “click-to-show name + distance” implementation.**  

---

## 1️⃣  Vector tiles vs. raw GeoJSON – which is the right choice?

| Feature | GeoJSON source (client-side) | Vector-tile source (server-side) |
|---------|-----------------------------|----------------------------------|
| **Data size** | 8 000 line features are usually **< 5 MB** (depends on geometry complexity). That can be loaded in one request, but the whole payload is parsed and kept in memory. | Only the tiles that intersect the current view are fetched. Even a 10-MB dataset can be delivered as a few hundred kilobytes per zoom level. |
| **Performance on zoom/pan** | Every pan triggers a full re-render of the whole dataset. At high zoom the browser must draw thousands of line segments each frame → noticeable lag on low-end devices. | Only the tiles that are visible are drawn. The GPU only sees the features that belong to those tiles, so panning/zooming stays buttery-smooth. |
| **Styling flexibility** | You can style with any MapLibre expression, but you cannot change the style without re-loading the whole source (unless you use `setPaintProperty`). | Styles are defined in the style JSON, not in the data. You can change line colour, width, dash-patterns, etc. on the fly with `setLayoutProperty`/`setPaintProperty` without touching the source. |
| **Interactivity (click, hover)** | Works out-of-the-box – each feature is a GeoJSON object with its properties. | Works out-of-the-box as well – MapLibre automatically “knows” which vector-tile feature was clicked. |
| **Server requirements** | None – just host a static `.geojson` file (or serve it via an API). | You need a tile-generation step (Tippecanoe, ogr2ogr → MBTiles, etc.) and a tile server (tileserver-gl, TileServer-PHP, Cloud-Optimized MBTiles, etc.) or a CDN that can serve XYZ/PMTiles. |
| **Future scaling** | Fine for a few thousand simple lines. Becomes a problem if you add more attributes, higher-resolution geometry, or want to support many concurrent users. | Scales to millions of features with no client-side penalty. |
| **Development effort** | Minimal – just `new maplibregl.GeoJSONSource({data: 'trails.geojson'})`. | Moderate – you need to generate tiles once, then add a `vector` source. The effort is usually a one-time build step. |

### Bottom line for **8 000 trail segments**

*If the GeoJSON file is **under ~3 MB** after you simplify the geometries (e.g., using `mapshaper -simplify 10%`), you can get away with a plain GeoJSON source, especially for a prototype or an internal tool.*  

*If you want a **production-ready, smooth experience on all devices**, or you anticipate adding more trails, elevation data, or richer attributes later, **go the vector-tile route**. The extra build step pays off in speed and scalability.*

---

## 2️⃣  Adding a terrain-and-roads basemap

You have two main options:

| Option | What you get | How to add it |
|--------|--------------|---------------|
| **Raster XYZ tiles** (e.g., OpenStreetMap + a separate terrain raster) | Simple, works everywhere, no style editing needed. | Add two `raster` sources (OSM roads, terrain hillshade) and stack them. |
| **Vector tiles + style** (e.g., MapTiler, OpenMapTiles, or your own vector basemap) | Full styling control, can add hillshade, 3-D terrain, label layers, etc. | Add a `vector` source (or use a pre-built style URL) and enable `terrain` with a DEM source. |

Below are concrete code snippets for both approaches. Choose the one that matches the data you already have or the provider you prefer.

### 2A️⃣  Raster basemap (quickest)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // 1️⃣ OpenStreetMap raster tiles (roads, labels)
      osm: {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: [
          'https://{a-c}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
        ],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
      },
      // 2️⃣ Terrain hillshade (optional – from MapTiler)
      hillshade: {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: [
          'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/hillshade/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
        ],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution: '&copy; MapTiler'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      { id: 'hillshade', type: 'raster', source: 'hillshade' },
      { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.45, 37.75],
  zoom: 10
});
```

*Pros*: No extra style work, works on any browser.  
*Cons*: No 3-D terrain, limited control over road styling.

### 2B️⃣  Vector basemap + DEM (recommended for a “real” map)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
  center: [-122.45, 37.75],
  zoom: 10,
  // Enable 3-D terrain (optional but looks great with hiking trails)
  terrain: {
    source: 'terrainSource',
    exaggeration: 1.5
  }
});

// Add a DEM (Digital Elevation Model) source for terrain
map.addSource('terrainSource', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});
```

*Why this works*:  
- The **MapTiler “streets” style** already contains roads, water, landcover, and a hillshade layer.  
- Adding a **raster-DEM source** lets you turn on `terrain` for true 3-D relief (useful for hikers).  
- You can still overlay your own trail layer on top of the vector basemap.

If you prefer an open-source vector basemap, you can host **OpenMapTiles** yourself (or use a free tier on a cloud provider). The style JSON will look similar; just replace the `style` URL.

---

## 3️⃣  Loading the trails & wiring up click-to-show name + distance

Below are two parallel implementations – one for a **GeoJSON source** and one for a **vector-tile source**. Pick the one that matches the decision you made in §1.

### 3A️⃣  Using a plain GeoJSON source

```js
// 1️⃣ Load the GeoJSON (you can also fetch it via AJAX and then `addSource` later)
map.addSource('trails', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/data/trails.geojson',
  // Optional: simplify on the fly (MapLibre 2.4+)
  // lineMetrics: true // needed for line-distance expression
});

// 2️⃣ Draw the lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'trails-line',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'trails',
  layout: {
    'line-cap': 'round',
    'line-join': 'round'
  },
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#006400',
    'line-width': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['zoom'],
      10, 2,
      14, 5
    ]
  }
});

// 3️⃣ Optional: pre-compute length (meters) and store it in a property.
// If you already have a `length_m` property in the GeoJSON, skip this step.
map.on('load', () => {
  const source = map.getSource('trails');
  fetch(source._data) // _data is the URL we gave above
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(geo => {
      geo.features.forEach(f => {
        // Turf.js makes this easy
        const length = turf.length(f, {units: 'kilometers'}); // km
        f.properties.length_m = length * 1000;
      });
      source.setData(geo);
    });
});

// 4️⃣ Click handler
map.on('click', 'trails-line', (e) => {
  const feature = e.features[0];
  const name = feature.properties.name || 'Unnamed trail';
  const length = feature.properties.length_m
    ? (feature.properties.length_m / 1000).toFixed(2) + ' km'
    : '—';

  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>Length: ${length}`)
    .addTo(map);
});

// 5️⃣ Change cursor on hover (nice UX)
map.on('mouseenter', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
map.on('mouseleave', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
```

**Key points**

- `lineMetrics: true` (or `line-distance` expression) is required if you want to compute the distance on the fly in the style.  
- Pre-computing the length with **turf.js** (or during your data-prep step) is far cheaper than asking the GPU to calculate it for every click.  
- The whole GeoJSON is loaded once, so the click handler is instantaneous after the initial load.

### 3B️⃣  Using vector tiles (recommended for production)

#### 3B-1️⃣  Generate the tiles (once)

```bash
# Install tippecanoe (Mac/Linux)
brew install tippecanoe   # macOS
# or apt-get install tippecanoe on Ubuntu

# Simplify & tile the GeoJSON (adjust --simplify-dp to keep enough detail)
tippecanoe \
  --output=trails.mbtiles \
  --layer=trails \
  --minimum-zoom=5 \
  --maximum-zoom=14 \
  --drop-densest-as-needed \
  --simplify=2 \
  trails.geojson
```

Upload `trails.mbtiles` to a tile server (e.g., **tileserver-gl**, **TileServer PHP**, **Cloudflare Workers + PMTiles**, or a static CDN that serves XYZ). The URL pattern will look like:

```
https://mytileserver.com/tiles/trails/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
```

#### 3B-2️⃣  Add the vector source & layer

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the vector source (replace URL with your own)
map.addSource('trails', {
  type: 'vector',
  // If you use a style JSON that already contains the source, you can skip this.
  url: 'https://mytileserver.com/tiles/trails.json' // or a TileJSON endpoint
});

// 2️⃣ Draw the lines (notice the "source-layer" name matches the layer you gave tippecanoe)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'trails-line',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'trails',
  'source-layer': 'trails', // <-- tippecanoe layer

### Short answer  
**No – you don’t need to run your own vector-tile server just to show ~50 store locations.**  
A lightweight client-side solution (GeoJSON + a public raster or vector tile basemap) will be faster to develop, cheaper to host, and more than sufficient for the performance you need.

Below is a quick decision-tree, followed by three practical implementation patterns you can copy-paste today, plus some “when you *might* want a self-hosted vector-tile server” notes.

---

## 1️⃣ Decision-tree (is a self-hosted vector-tile server worth it?)

| Situation | Recommended approach |
|-----------|----------------------|
| **Only a few dozen points** (≤ ~200) and you just need to show them on a map | **Client-side GeoJSON** + any public basemap (raster or vector). |
| **You already have a map-style platform** (Mapbox, Maptiler, CARTO, etc.) | Use their **hosted vector tiles** and add your points as a separate GeoJSON layer. |
| **You need custom basemap styling** that you can’t achieve with the hosted style editor | Use a **hosted vector-tile service** (Mapbox, Maptiler, OpenMapTiles Cloud) and edit the style there – still no server to run yourself. |
| **You need offline maps** (e.g., a native mobile app that works without internet) | Consider **pre-packaged MBTiles** (vector or raster) that you ship with the app. |
| **You expect the data to grow to thousands of points, need heavy clustering, or want to serve many concurrent users** | A **self-hosted vector-tile server** (e.g., TileServer-GL, Tileserver-PHP, or a PostGIS + pg_tileserv stack) becomes worthwhile. |
| **You have strict licensing or privacy constraints** (e.g., you can’t send store coordinates to a third-party) | Self-hosted vector tiles (or just serve GeoJSON from your own API) solves that. |

For a typical store-locator website with ~50 locations, you’ll fall into the first two rows – no need to spin up a tile server.

---

## 2️⃣ Three “quick-start” patterns

### A. **Leaflet + raster OSM tiles + GeoJSON layer** (simplest, works everywhere)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Store Locator</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
  <style>#map { height: 100vh; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script>
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map
const map = L.map('map').setView([39.5, -98.35], 4); // continental US centre

// 2️⃣ Add a free raster basemap (OpenStreetMap)
L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors',
  maxZoom: 19,
}).addTo(map);

// 3️⃣ Your store data (GeoJSON).  Replace with your real coordinates.
const stores = {
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "features": [
    { "type":"Feature","properties":{"name":"Store A","address":"123 Main St"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-122.4194,37.7749]}},
    { "type":"Feature","properties":{"name":"Store B","address":"456 Oak Ave"},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-74.0060,40.7128]}},
    // … add the rest (≈50)
  ]
};

// 4️⃣ Render the points
L.geoJSON(stores, {
  pointToLayer: (feature, latlng) => L.marker(latlng),
  onEachFeature: (feature, layer) => {
    const {name, address} = feature.properties;
    layer.bindPopup(`<strong>${name}</strong><br>${address}`);
  }
}).addTo(map);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Why this works for 50 points**

* No extra server work – the GeoJSON can be a static file or served from a tiny API.
* Leaflet handles clustering automatically if you later need it (`leaflet.markercluster`).
* Raster tiles are cached by browsers/CDNs, so performance is excellent.

---

### B. **MapLibre GL JS + hosted vector tiles + GeoJSON source** (if you want a modern “Mapbox-style” look)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Store Locator – MapLibre GL</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { height: 100vh; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map with a public vector-tile style (Maptiler, OpenMapTiles, etc.)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY', // <-- replace
  center: [-98.35, 39.5],
  zoom: 4
});

// 2️⃣ Add the store points as a GeoJSON source once the style loads
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addSource('stores', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: 'stores.geojson'   // static file or your API endpoint
  });

  // 3️⃣ Render them as circles (you can switch to icons if you like)
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'store-circles',
    type: 'circle',
    source: 'stores',
    paint: {
      'circle-radius': 8,
      'circle-color': '#ff6600',
      'circle-stroke-width': 2,
      'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
    }
  });

  // 4️⃣ Popup on click
  map.on('click', 'store-circles', (e) => {
    const props = e.features[0].properties;
    new maplibregl.Popup()
      .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
      .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong><br>${props.address}`)
      .addTo(map);
  });

  // Change cursor on hover
  map.on('mouseenter', 'store-circles', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'store-circles', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Key points**

* **Vector basemap** is served by a third-party (Maptiler, OpenMapTiles Cloud, etc.) – you only pay for the style API, not for a full tile server.
* Your store data stays a tiny GeoJSON file (≈ few KB).  
* You get smooth zoom-dependent styling, 3-D terrain, etc., “for free”.

---

### C. **Self-hosted static vector tiles (MBTiles) + MapLibre** (offline or privacy-first)

If you *must* keep everything on your own domain (e.g., corporate policy), you can ship a pre-generated MBTiles file (≈ 30 MB for a continental-US basemap at zoom 0-12) and serve it with a tiny static-file server (NGINX, Cloudflare Workers, GitHub Pages). No “tile server” process is required.

1. **Generate the MBTiles** (once) with the open-source **OpenMapTiles** pipeline or use the ready-made `openmaptiles/tileset` on Docker Hub.

   ```bash
   docker run -v $(pwd):/data -e TILESET=planet \
     -e BOUNDS="-180,-85,180,85" \
     -e MIN_ZOOM=0 -e MAX_ZOOM=12 \
     openmaptiles/tileset \
     generate --output /data/us.mbtiles
   ```

2. **Serve the MBTiles** with a static-file server that supports range requests (required for vector tiles). Example using **Cloudflare Workers** (free tier):

   ```js
   // worker.js
   addEventListener('fetch', event => {
     event.respondWith(handleRequest(event.request))
   })

   async function handleRequest(request) {
     const url = new URL(request.url)
     // Expected pattern: /tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
     const match = url.pathname.match(/\/tiles\/(\d+)\/(\d+)\/(\d+)\.pbf/)
     if (!match) return fetch(request) // fallback to other assets

     const [_, z, x, y] = match
     const tileKey = `${z}/${x}/${y}`
     const mbtiles = await MBTiles.open('us.mbtiles') // use a library like @mapbox/mbtiles
     const tile = await mbtiles.getTile(z, x, y)
     return new Response(tile, {
       headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-protobuf' }
     })
   }
   ```

3. **Reference the local tileset** in your MapLibre style:

   ```json
   {
     "version": 8,
     "sources": {
       "openmaptiles": {
         "type": "vector",
         "url": "mbtiles://{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
       }
     },
     "layers": [ /* your usual style layers */ ]
   }
   ```

> **Result:** You have a *static* vector-tile endpoint that behaves like a tile server but requires no dynamic rendering. It’s perfect for a small site that wants full control without the operational overhead of a full-blown TileServer-GL.

---

## 3️⃣ Why a full-blown vector-tile server is usually overkill for 50 points

| Factor | Typical cost/complexity for a self-hosted server | What you actually need for 50 points |
|--------|---------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| **Infrastructure** | A VM/container, ~2 CPU, 2 GB RAM, storage for the tileset (tens of GB for US-wide vector tiles). | A static web server (or CDN) that can serve a few KB of GeoJSON. |
| **Ops overhead** | Monitoring, TLS, tile-cache invalidation, log rotation, scaling. | Deploy once, maybe a tiny cron to refresh the GeoJSON if stores change. |
| **Performance** | Overkill – the server can serve millions of tiles/second, but you’ll only request a handful per user session. | Browser can render 50 points instantly; network payload < 10 KB. |
| **Cost** | $10-$30 / month for a small cloud VM + bandwidth, plus any commercial tile-provider fees if you use their style. | Free (OpenStreetMap raster tiles) or a low-cost hosted vector style (Maptiler $5-$10 / month). |
| **Future-proofing** | Good if you plan to expand to thousands of points, custom basemap layers, or offline mobile apps. | Not needed now; you can always add a tile server later without rewriting the UI. |

---

## 4️⃣ Practical tips for a smooth store-locator experience

| Feature | How to implement (with the patterns above) |
|---------|--------------------------------------------|
| **Search / autocomplete** | Use a lightweight geocoding API (Mapbox Geocoding, Pelias, or the free **OpenStreetMap Nominatim** instance) to turn an address into a lat/lng, then `map.flyTo({center, zoom: 14})`. |
| **Clustering** (if you ever exceed ~200 points) | Leaflet: `L.markerClusterGroup()`; MapLibre: `supercluster` library + a `circle` layer that aggregates points. |
| **Responsive design** | Set the map container to `height: 100vh` on mobile, or use CSS grid to place a list of stores beside the map on larger screens. |
| **Accessibility** | Add `aria-label` to the map container, ensure popups are keyboard-focusable, and provide a plain-text list of locations as a fallback. |
| **Analytics** | Track clicks on store markers (`map.on('click', ...)`) to see which locations draw interest. |
| **Data updates** | Store the GeoJSON in a version-controlled repo (Git) and deploy

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for putting a scanned 1920 s map (or any georeferenced raster image) on a **MapLibre GL JS** map when you know the latitude/longitude of the four corners.  
It also shows a simple UI that lets the user turn the overlay on/off and slide its opacity.

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **The image** (JPG, PNG, WebP, …) | Must be reachable by the browser (same-origin or CORS-enabled). |
| **Four corner coordinates** | In *lon, lat* order, clockwise starting at the top-left corner (the order MapLibre expects). |
| **MapLibre GL JS** (≥ v2.0) | The `image` source type is part of the style spec. |
| **A tiny bit of HTML/JS** | To add the source, the raster layer, and the UI controls. |

---

## 2. The style spec you’ll use

```json
{
  "type": "image",
  "url": "https://example.com/old-city-map.jpg",
  "coordinates": [
    [lonTL, latTL],   // top-left
    [lonTR, latTR],   // top-right
    [lonBR, latBR],   // bottom-right
    [lonBL, latBL]    // bottom-left
  ]
}
```

*`coordinates`* are **lon/lat** (not lat/lon) and are interpreted in **Web Mercator (EPSG:3857)** – the same projection MapLibre uses internally.  
If your source image is not perfectly rectangular on the ground (e.g., it was scanned slightly skewed), you can still correct it by tweaking these four points.

---

## 3. Full working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – historic map overlay</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .control-panel {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 8px 12px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
      font-size: 13px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<div class="control-panel">
  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" id="toggleOverlay" checked>
    Show historic map
  </label>
  <br>
  Opacity:
  <input type="range" id="opacitySlider" min="0" max="100" value="80">
</div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the base map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // OpenStreetMap raster tiles (you can swap this for any vector style you like)
        osm: {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: [
            'https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://b.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://c.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'osm-basemap',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0], // will be overwritten once the overlay loads
    zoom: 2
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the corner coordinates of your scanned map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Replace these with the real numbers you have.
  // Order: top-left, top-right, bottom-right, bottom-left
  const overlayCorners = [
    [-122.447303, 37.768390], // top-left
    [-122.445500, 37.768390], // top-right
    [-122.445500, 37.766800], // bottom-right
    [-122.447303, 37.766800]  // bottom-left
  ];

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Add the image source + raster layer once the map is ready
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // 3a – add the image source
    map.addSource('historic-map', {
      type: 'image',
      url: 'https://example.com/old-city-map.jpg', // <-- change to your URL
      coordinates: overlayCorners
    });

    // 3b – add a raster layer that draws the image
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historic-map-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historic-map',
      paint: {
        // start with 80 % opacity (0-1 range)
        'raster-opacity': 0.8
      }
    });

    // 3c – optionally zoom the view to the overlay bounds
    const bounds = overlayCorners.reduce((b, coord) => b.extend(coord), new maplibregl.LngLatBounds());
    map.fitBounds(bounds, { padding: 30 });
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  UI: toggle visibility & change opacity
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const toggle = document.getElementById('toggleOverlay');
  const opacitySlider = document.getElementById('opacitySlider');

  // Visibility checkbox
  toggle.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    const visibility = e.target.checked ? 'visible' : 'none';
    map.setLayoutProperty('historic-map-layer', 'visibility', visibility);
  });

  // Opacity slider (0-100 → 0-1)
  opacitySlider.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
    const opacity = Number(e.target.value) / 100;
    map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Section | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| **Base map** | Any raster or vector style works; the example uses OSM raster tiles. |
| **`overlayCorners`** | The four corner points you already measured. Keep the order **top-left → top-right → bottom-right → bottom-left**. |
| **`map.addSource('historic-map', {type:'image', …})`** | Registers the scanned map as an *image source*. The `url` can be a relative path, a CDN URL, or a data-URI. |
| **`map.addLayer({type:'raster', source:'historic-map', …})`** | Draws the image on the map. The raster layer is the only layer type that can consume an `image` source. |
| **`fitBounds`** | Zooms the map so the whole historic map is visible when the page loads. |
| **Visibility toggle** | Uses `setLayoutProperty(..., 'visibility', ...)` to hide/show the overlay instantly. |
| **Opacity slider** | Calls `setPaintProperty(..., 'raster-opacity', …)`; values are 0 → 1. |

---

## 4. Things to watch out for

| Issue | How to avoid / fix |
|-------|--------------------|
| **CORS** | The image must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain). If you host it yourself, add that header. If you can’t change the server, copy the file to a location you control (e.g., your own static-file bucket). |
| **Wrong coordinate order** | MapLibre expects **lon, lat** (not lat, lon) and the clockwise order shown above. Swapped axes or a different winding will rotate/flip the image. |
| **Image size vs. map resolution** | The raster layer is rendered at the map’s current pixel density. Very large JPGs (several MB) can slow the browser. If the file is huge, consider down-sampling it to something like 2000 px on the longest side – the visual quality of a 1920 s map is usually fine at that size. |
| **Retina / high-DPI displays** | The raster layer automatically scales with the device pixel ratio, so you don’t need a separate @2x image. |
| **Changing the map projection** | MapLibre only works in Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). If you need a different projection you’d have to re-project the image yourself (e.g., with GDAL) before using it. |
| **Future edits** | If you later want to let the user move/rotate the historic map interactively, you can replace the static `image` source with a **`canvas` source** and draw the image onto a canvas that you transform with `map.transform`. That’s more advanced, but the static source is the simplest and most performant for a fixed overlay. |

---

## 5. Optional enhancements

| Feature | Quick implementation tip |
|---------|--------------------------|
| **Layer switcher UI** | Add a `<select>` with multiple historic maps, each with its own source/layer, and call `map.setLayoutProperty(id, 'visibility', …)` when the user picks one. |
| **Opacity persistence** | Store the slider value in `localStorage` and read it back on page load. |
| **Legend / attribution** | Append a small `<div>` with the map’s title and source (e.g., “City Archive, 1924”). |
| **Clickable info** | Add a transparent `fill` layer on top of the raster and use `map.on('click', …)` to show pop-ups for historic landmarks. |
| **Export as PDF** | Use `map.getCanvas().toBlob()` after the user has positioned the overlay, then feed the blob to a PDF library (jsPDF) if you need printable copies. |

---

## 6. TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

If you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map` and you just want the overlay:

```js
// 1️⃣  Add the source (replace URL & corners)
map.addSource('historic', {
  type: 'image',
  url: 'https://example.com/old-map.jpg',
  coordinates: [
    [lonTL, latTL],
    [lonTR, latTR],
    [lonBR, latBR],
    [lonBL, latBL]
  ]
});

// 2️⃣  Add the raster layer
map.addLayer({
  id: 'historic-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'historic',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.8 }
});

// 3️⃣  Opacity UI (example)
document.getElementById('opacity').addEventListener('input', e => {
  map.setPaintProperty('historic-layer', 'raster-opacity', e.target.value / 100);
});
```

That’s all you need to get a 1920 s scanned map sitting perfectly on top of a modern MapLibre map, with a user-controllable opacity toggle. Happy mapping!

## TL;DR  
Yes – you can display a **MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap** inside a Leaflet map. The easiest way is to use the tiny bridge library **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** (or its newer sibling **`@maplibre/leaflet`**). It creates a Leaflet overlay that internally runs a MapLibre GL JS map, so you get the full power of a GL style (layers, filters, 3-D terrain, etc.) while keeping the rest of your app in plain Leaflet.

Below is a step-by-step guide, from installing the libraries to a working code sandbox, plus tips, alternatives, and common pitfalls.

---

## 1. What’s actually happening?

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Leaflet** | Handles the UI you already have – pan/zoom, markers, pop-ups, custom controls, etc. |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Renders the vector-tile basemap using a MapLibre style JSON (the same format used by Mapbox GL). |
| **maplibre-gl-leaflet** (or `@maplibre/leaflet`) | A thin wrapper that creates a Leaflet `Layer` whose `onAdd`/`onRemove` lifecycle hooks instantiate a hidden MapLibre GL map and sync its view with the Leaflet map. |

The result is a **single Leaflet map object** (`L.map`) that you can treat exactly like any other Leaflet map, but one of its layers is a fully-styled vector tile layer.

---

## 2. Install the required libraries

You can use a CDN for quick prototyping or npm/yarn for a production build.

### CDN (quick demo)

```html
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
/>
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
/>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/leaflet@0.0.5/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Why `@maplibre/leaflet`?**  
> The original `maplibre-gl-leaflet` repo is now archived. The community-maintained `@maplibre/leaflet` package (v0.0.x) is a drop-in replacement and works with MapLibre GL ≥ 4.0.

### npm / Yarn (production)

```bash
# Leaflet + MapLibre GL + the bridge
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl @maplibre/leaflet
# or with yarn
yarn add leaflet maplibre-gl @maplibre/leaflet
```

Then import in your bundler (ESM, CommonJS, Vite, etc.):

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';

import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import { MapLibreLayer } from '@maplibre/leaflet';
```

---

## 3. Get a MapLibre style JSON + vector tile source

You need a **style** that points to a vector-tile endpoint (e.g., `https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` is raster, not vector). Some ready-made options:

| Provider | URL (style JSON) | Tile endpoint (vector) | Notes |
|----------|------------------|------------------------|-------|
| **OpenMapTiles** (free tier) | `https://free.tilehosting.com/data/v3.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | `https://free.tilehosting.com/data/v3/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf?key=YOUR_KEY` | Requires an API key (free up to 100 k tiles/mo). |
| **MapTiler** | `https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/v3/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf?key=YOUR_KEY` | Free tier 100 k tiles/mo, includes satellite, terrain, etc. |
| **Planetiler / OSM2VectorTiles** | Self-hosted style JSON | Self-hosted vector tiles | Full control, but you need to run a tile server. |
| **MapLibre Demo Styles** | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json` | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` | Public demo, no token, great for testing. |

> **Tip:** For a quick start, use the demo style above. When you go to production, replace it with a style you control (MapTiler, OpenMapTiles, or your own).

---

## 4. Minimal working example

Below is a complete HTML page that shows a Leaflet map with a MapLibre vector basemap, plus a regular Leaflet marker to prove the two worlds coexist.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL Vector Basemap</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <!-- Leaflet CSS -->
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
  />
  <!-- MapLibre GL CSS -->
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
  />
  <style>
    html,
    body,
    #map {
      height: 100%;
      margin: 0;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>

  <!-- Leaflet JS -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <!-- Bridge library -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/leaflet@0.0.5/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Initialise the Leaflet map (any CRS works – we use the default EPSG:3857)
    const map = L.map('map', {
      center: [51.505, -0.09],
      zoom: 13,
      // optional: make the GL layer the base (no background tiles)
      // you can also set `preferCanvas: true` if you need canvas fallback
    });

    // 2️⃣ Create the MapLibre GL layer
    const glLayer = new L.MapLibreGL({
      // The style JSON – you can also fetch it dynamically with fetch()
      style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
      // Optional: pass any MapLibre GL options here (e.g., token, attribution)
      // Example: accessToken: 'YOUR_TOKEN', // not needed for demo style
      // If you want to enable terrain, add a terrain source in the style JSON
    });

    // 3️⃣ Add the GL layer to the Leaflet map
    glLayer.addTo(map);

    // 4️⃣ Add a regular Leaflet marker to prove interop
    L.marker([51.505, -0.09])
      .addTo(map)
      .bindPopup('I am a Leaflet marker!')
      .openPopup();

    // 5️⃣ (Optional) Add a Leaflet control for attribution
    map.attributionControl.addAttribution(
      '&copy; <a href="https://openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
    );
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What you’ll see**

* The background is the MapLibre vector basemap (streets, water, etc.).
* A classic Leaflet marker sits on top, with a popup that works exactly as before.
* Zooming/panning updates both the GL layer and the Leaflet overlay in sync.

---

## 5. Full-featured setup (real-world considerations)

Below is a more production-ready snippet that shows:

* **Token handling** (MapTiler, OpenMapTiles, etc.).
* **Custom attribution**.
* **Layer ordering** (putting the GL layer *below* other Leaflet overlays).
* **Enabling 3-D terrain** (if your style includes a terrain source).
* **Responsive handling of high-DPI screens**.

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { MapLibreLayer } from '@maplibre/leaflet';

// 1️⃣ Leaflet map
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.006], // New York
  zoom: 12,
  // Ensure the GL layer is rendered *below* other Leaflet layers
  // (Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added)
});

// 2️⃣ MapLibre GL layer options
const glOptions = {
  // Use your own style URL (replace with your token if needed)
  style:
    'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
  // If you need to pass extra MapLibre GL options:
  //   hash: true,
  //   transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => {
  //     // Example: add auth header for private tiles
  //     if (resourceType === 'Source' && url.includes('my-private-tiles')) {
  //       return {
  //         url,
  //         headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer MY_JWT' },
  //       };
  //     }
  //   },
  //   // Enable 3-D terrain if the style references a terrain source
  //   terrain: {
  //     source: 'mapbox-dem',
  //     exaggeration: 1.5,
  //   },
};

// 3️⃣ Create the layer and add it *first* (so it becomes the base)
const glLayer = new MapLibreLayer(glOptions);
glLayer.addTo(map);

// 4️⃣ Add any Leaflet overlays *after* the GL layer
L.marker([40.7128, -74.006])
  .addTo(map)
  .bindPopup('Leaflet marker on top of GL basemap');

// 5️⃣ Attribution (MapLibre GL does not add its own attribution automatically)
map.attributionControl.addAttribution(
  '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors ' +
    '| Tiles &amp; style by <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a>'
);
```

### Key points in the code above

| Feature | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Layer order** | Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added. Add the GL layer first, then any Leaflet overlays (markers, GeoJSON, etc.). |
| **Token** | Most hosted vector-tile services require a query-string token (`?key=…`). Just embed it in the style URL; the bridge forwards the request unchanged. |
| **Terrain** | If your style JSON contains a `terrain` source (e.g., Mapbox DEM), you can enable it via the `terrain` option. The bridge passes it straight to MapLibre GL. |
| **High-DPI** | MapLibre GL automatically requests `@2x` tiles when `window.devicePixelRatio > 1`. No extra work needed. |
| **Custom request handling** | Use `transformRequest` (MapLibre GL option) to add auth headers, change URLs, or proxy requests. The bridge forwards the function unchanged. |

---

## 6. Alternative approaches (if you don’t want the bridge)

| Approach | When it makes sense |
|----------|--------------------|
| **Pure MapLibre GL JS + Leaflet controls** | If you only need a basemap and can rewrite UI in GL (e.g., use `maplibre-gl` for everything). |
| **`leaflet.vectorgrid` (L.VectorGrid.Protobuf)** | Renders vector tiles directly in Leaflet (Canvas or SVG). Good for simple styling, but you lose the full MapLibre style language (expressions, filters, 3-D). |
| **`mapbox-gl-leaflet` (deprecated)** | Older wrapper for Mapbox GL JS; still works but is tied to Mapbox’s proprietary token system. |
| **`react-leaflet` + `react-map-gl` side-by-side** | In React apps you can render two maps stacked with CSS, syncing their view manually. More complex, usually unnecessary. |

**Why
